<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mayenne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mayenne is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the Pays de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> region, named after the river Mayenne that crosses it from north to south. Its capital is Laval. The area corresponds broadly to the lands of the Gallic Diablintes and later formed part of the provinces of Maine and Anjou.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mayenne.jpg" alt="Mayenne" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Celeste (permission given to Oie blanche) · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pr%C3%A9fecture_Laval.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> lies in the Pays de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> region and has no sea coast, though Mont Saint-Michel Bay is about thirty kilometres to the north-west. It is roughly rectangular, 90 km long and 77 km wide, with an area of about 5,175 km2. The Manche, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarthe">Sarthe</a>, Maine-et-Loire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ille-et-vilaine">Ille-et-Vilaine</a> departments surround it. The river Mayenne flows through its centre from north to south, passing the towns of Mayenne, Laval and Château-Gontier, and then joins the Sarthe to form the Maine, which flows into the Loire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mayenne is one of the original 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, created on 4 March 1790 by the National Constituent Assembly during the early stages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>. The new departments were meant to be uniform in administration and roughly equal in size and population. The old province of Maine was divided in two: Upper Maine, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarthe">Sarthe</a>, and Lower Maine, centred on Laval, became Mayenne. Anjou was too large for a single department and was cut down to form Maine-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, with Mayenne receiving Château-Gontier and Craon.</p><p>The border between Mayenne and Maine-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> followed the southern limit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> cultivation, since flax was a feature of the local economy. The northern two thirds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> therefore correspond to western Maine, and the southern third to the northern part of Anjou. The department is also coextensive with the Roman Catholic Diocese of Laval.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> lies on the Armorican Massif, where the Mont des Avaloirs, at 417 m, is the highest point. The rest is largely flat, with some hilly areas and steep-sided valleys. Laval is the prefecture and largest town, and the department is divided into three arrondissements: Mayenne, Laval and Château-Gontier. It had 305,468 inhabitants in 2023, who are called Mayennais.</p><p>About 80 per cent of the land is farmed, and livestock rearing, above all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, horses and pigs, predominates. Apple orchards supply a traditional cider industry. Iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> have been mined, and quarries yield marble, slate, limestone and flint. Industries include linen, paper and hemp, office furniture at Château-Gontier, and dairy products and electronics at Laval.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mayenne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the French Wars of Religion, Henry IV, leading French royal and English forces, won a decisive victory over the Catholic League under the Duke of <b>Mayenne</b> and Spanish forces under the Count of Egmont.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-14">14 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aulerci were a group of related peoples in north-western Gaul; the Diablintes held the area around Jublains in <b>Mayenne</b>, between the Cenomani and the Redones.</blockquote><a href="/state/aulerci-diablintes">Aulerci Diablintes</a></li></ul></section>
